 step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nτ_{n} &= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - y_{new} \\\\\n&= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - (y_{exact}(x) + Δy)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\ny_{exact}^{′}(x) = (x^{3})^{′} = 3x^{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{exact}^{′}(x) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{exact}(x_{new}) &= y_{exact}(x + Δx) \\\\\n&= (x + Δx)^{3} \\\\\n&= x^{3} + 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx + 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nτ_{n} &= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - (y_{exact}(x) + Δy) \\\\\n&= [x^{3} + 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx + 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}] - [x^{3} + 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx] \\\\\n&= x^{3} - x^{3} + (3x^{2} - 3x^{2}) \\cdot Δx + 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3} \\\\\n&= 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534973 false] [17592186166443 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}]-[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{3}+(3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3} \\\\  & =3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534973 true] [17592186166444 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x) = \\sqrt{x + 2}$, find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ |  |" 13194139534973 false] [17592186166444 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x) = \\sqrt{x + 2},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ |  |" 13194139534973 true] [17592186166444 1145 "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x_{n-1}$ to\n\n$$\nx_n = x_{n-1} + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ne_n = y(x_n) - y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $y(x_n)$ is the exact value at $x_n$, and $y_n$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values $y_n$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y(x_n)$ at each value of $x_n{:}$\n\n- The exact value at $x_0=0$ is $y(0) = \\sqrt{0 + 2} = \\sqrt{2}$.\n- The exact value at $x_1=0.3$ is $y(0.3) = \\sqrt{0.3 + 2}$.\n- The exact value at $x_2=0.6$ is $y(0.6) = \\sqrt{0.6 + 2}$.\n- The exact value at $x_3=0.9$ is $y(0.9) = \\sqrt{0.9 + 2}$.\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n$e_{1}|= y(x_{1}) - y_{1}; = \\sqrt{2.3} - 1.480; \\approx 0.037$.\n- The GTE of the second step is\n$e_{2}|= y(x_{2}) - y_{2}; = \\sqrt{2.6} - 1.570; \\approx 0.042$.\n- The GTE of the third step is\n$e_{3}|= y(x_{3}) - y_{3}; = \\sqrt{2.9} - 1.650; \\approx 0.053$.\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.037}$ |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.042}$ |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.053}$ |" 13194139534973 false] [17592186166444 1145 "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $