lobal-truncation-errors\"></a>" 13194139534973 false] [17592186167141 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)={e}^{-x},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139534973 true] [17592186167141 1145 "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)={e}^{-0}=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.2$ is $y(0.2)={e}^{-0.2}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.4$ is $y(0.4)={e}^{-0.4}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.6$ is $y(0.6)={e}^{-0.6}.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | ${e}^{-0.2}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | ${e}^{-0.4}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | ${e}^{-0.6}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.2}-0.762 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.4}-0.558 \\\\  & ≈0.112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.6}-0.353 \\\\  & ≈0.196.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | ${e}^{-0.2}$ | $0.057$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | ${e}^{-0.4}$ | $0.112$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | ${e}^{-0.6}$ | $0.196$ |" 13194139534973 true] [17592186167146 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534973 true] [17592186167146 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAn initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{exact}(x) = \\sin x$. A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $τ_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value.\n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $τ_{n}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.250$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.483$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.688$ | {{field-3}} |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"computing-the-lte-for-a-numerical-method-given-an-initial-value-problem\"></a>" 13194139534973 false] [17592186167146 1100 "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n