t{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.22$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.117.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.22)-1.117 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.22}-1.117 \\\\  & ≈-0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.44$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.185.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.44)-1.185 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.44}-1.185 \\\\  & =0.015.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.66$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.299.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.66)-1.299 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.66}-1.299 \\\\ = & ≈-0.011.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.22$ | $1.117$ | $-0.012$ |\n| $2$ | $0.44$ | $1.185$ | $0.015$ |\n| $3$ | $0.66$ | $1.299$ | $-0.011$ |" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323861 13194139534973 true] [17592186323860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323862 13194139534973 true] [17592186323860 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534973 true] [17592186323860 1041 "field-1" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323860 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323862 13194139534973 true] [17592186323860 1110 #uuid "2c1f9e74-073b-5a69-8c8d-8e76148ba4cd" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323861 1034 "0.012" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323861 1111 #uuid "7f2fb74e-4f3b-55a4-827e-4fdcc86d0172" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323862 1034 "-0.012" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323862 1111 #uuid "58a5c66b-378d-5c5b-86d6-dd6b9e3addae" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323863 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323864 13194139534973 true] [17592186323863 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534973 true] [17592186323863 1041 "field-2" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323863 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323864 13194139534973 true] [17592186323863 1110 #uuid "48e20bab-b1ce-528e-87f3-0a21b0ac62cf" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323864 1034 "0.015" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323864 1111 #uuid "c85bbce3-048f-5dc2-9531-0475275d90a4" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323866 13194139534973 true] [17592186323865 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534973 true] [17592186323865 1041 "field-3" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323865 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323866 13194139534973 true] [17592186323865 1110 #uuid "def4ed84-067c-5153-affa-bb7bce6530d5" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323866 1034 "-0.011" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323866 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323866 1111 #uuid "edc24d12-5f7d-5e7a-8f31-9db032a00e92" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323867 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323868 13194139534973 true] [17592186323867 1089 "q-333255" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323867 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534973 true] [17592186323867 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{3x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{1}$ for the first step starting at $x=0$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323867 1113 #uuid "5d307a29-b06f-559a-9712-6b37b8d54e46" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323867 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact $y$-value at the start of the step $x=0$ is $y(0)=1.$  Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0) & =3{y}_{\\text{exact}}(0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.2) \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0.2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the first step starting at $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-(y(0)+Δy) \\\\  & ={e}^{0.6}-(1+0.6) \\\\  & ={e}^{0.6}-1.6 \\\\  & ≈0.222\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323869 13194139534973 true] [17592186323868 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534973 true] [17592186323868 1041 "field-1" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323868 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323869 13194139534973 true] [17592186323868 1110 #uuid "06e7f6e3-e961-5d60-9037-4f3daa7fa393" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323869 1034 "0.222" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323869 1111 #uuid "a72489a6-97ab-5658-8d66-72775e54d708" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323870 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323871 13194139534973 true] [17592186323870 1089 "q-333258" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323870 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534973 true] [17592186323870 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+3,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+3x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{1},$ for the first step starting at $x=0$ with step size $Δx=0.1.$\n\n${τ}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323870 1113 #uuid "06b84e8c-4740-5260-901a-5b6c0f03dd38" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323870 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact $y$-value at the start of the step $x=0$ is $y(0)=0.$ Now, let's compute the \n$y$-increment using the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0) & =2(0)+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x\\cdot {y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0)+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3+3)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0+0.1) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)^{2}+3(0.1) \\\\  & =0.01+0.3 \\\\  & =0.31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the first step starting at $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-(y(0)+Δy) \\\\  & =0.31-(0+0.3) \\\\  & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323872 13194139534973 true] [17592186323871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323873 13194139534973 true] [17592186323871 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534973 true] [17592186323871 1041 "field-1" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323871 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323873 13194139534973 true] [17592186323871 1110 #uuid "c14762d6-a43b-5ab2-83a4-1528c768a196" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323872 1034 "-0.01" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323872 1111 #uuid "6d1706b6-0fed-5bf8-96ce-75e5ae9dd55f" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323873 1034 "0.01" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323873 1111 #uuid "a2f53daa-0f06-50be-9aed-0aee325b83ac" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323875 13194139534973 true] [17592186323874 1089 "q-333384" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323874 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534973 true] [17592186323874 1100 "Consider the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nSuppose we approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{6}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323874 1113 #uuid "fb1dc8bb-2bde-5237-aebc-5fd45ae2416d" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323874 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=3{e}^{-1}.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =-{y}_{\\text{exact}}(1) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =-0.6{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-(3{e}^{-1}-0.6{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-2.4{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.021\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323876 13194139534973 true] [17592186323875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323877 13194139534973 true] [17592186323875 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534973 true] [17592186323875 1041 "field-1" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323875 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323877 13194139534973 true] [17592186323875 1110 #uuid "ae1c8b14-e957-593b-a349-a33edb04e876" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323876 1034 "-0.021" 13194139534973 true] [17592186323876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534973 true] [17592186323876 1111 #uuid "15edbd2d-edeb-589a-8a60-cd3f5cb2be49" 13194139534973 true] [17592